# Scribe demo configuration.
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. NEVER commit the filled-in file — `.env` is gitignored.
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# One file drives everything: every backend reads it, the SPA reads it (Vite is pointed here
# with `envDir: ".."`), and `make` and `tests/contract/run.sh` source it. Nothing in the demo
# reads a credential from anywhere but the environment.


# ==============================================================================
# Meteroid credentials — required
# ==============================================================================

# Server-side Meteroid API key, sent as `Authorization: Bearer <key>`.
# Never exposed to the browser: the SPA only ever talks to the demo backend.
METEROID_API_KEY=

# Meteroid API base URL. Leave as-is for the hosted API; point it at your own
# instance for local development.
METEROID_BASE_URL=https://api.meteroid.com

# Signing secret of the webhook endpoint you created in the Meteroid dashboard
# (CATALOG.md section 6). Starts with `whsec_`.
#
# Two things use it: the backends VERIFY inbound webhooks with it, and the contract
# suite SIGNS its own test payloads with it — which is how the webhook receiver is
# tested with no live tenant and no public tunnel. Unset, the receiver returns 500
# and the suite skips its 17 webhook tests.
METEROID_WEBHOOK_SECRET=


# ==============================================================================
# Demo backend
# ==============================================================================

# HMAC key for demo session tokens. NOT a Meteroid credential — it signs the opaque
# token the SPA holds. Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Required: every backend refuses to start without it, because a predictable default
# would let anyone mint a token for any workspace.
#
# Session tokens are a stateless HMAC over the customer alias, so the same secret
# across backends means one token works against all of them.
SCRIBE_SESSION_SECRET=

# Currency for the customers the demo creates. MUST match the currency of the seeded
# plans, or checkout fails with a currency mismatch.
SCRIBE_DEFAULT_CURRENCY=USD

# Listen port.
#
# DELIBERATELY LEFT UNSET. Each backend has its own default so they can run side by
# side out of the box:
#
#     rust        8080
#     java        8081
#     typescript  8082
#     python      8083
#     go          8084
#
# Setting SCRIBE_PORT here would apply it to *whichever* backend you start and put
# two of them on the same port. Every backend reads `PORT` first, then `SCRIBE_PORT`,
# so override per process instead — which is exactly what `make run-java` does:
#
#     PORT=9000 make run-rust
#
# SCRIBE_PORT=


# ==============================================================================
# Frontend
# ==============================================================================

# Which backend the SPA talks to. Change this to point the same SPA at a different
# implementation; nothing else about the frontend changes.
#
# Keep it in step with BASE_URL below — `tests/e2e` seeds the SPA's session token into
# localStorage under a key namespaced by this URL, so if the two disagree the browser
# and the tests are driving two different backends.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080


# ==============================================================================
# Tests — all optional
# ==============================================================================

# Which backend the suites drive. Run the contract suite once per backend.
# `make test-contract BACKEND=java` sets this for you.
BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080

# Assert `GET /api/health` reports this implementation ("rust" | "java" | "typescript" | "python" | "go").
# Worth setting in CI so a mis-pointed BASE_URL fails loudly instead of passing.
# SCRIBE_EXPECT_BACKEND=

# A session token for a workspace that ALREADY has a subscription.
#
# Both test suites need one and neither can create it: reaching a subscribed state
# means completing a hosted Meteroid checkout, which is a payment form on Meteroid's
# own domain. So an operator subscribes one workspace by hand and exports its token.
# Tokens are stateless HMACs and do not expire, so one fixture serves every run.
#
# Point it at a workspace on a plan with a FINITE limit (Free, limit 30) — the quota
# tests are unreachable on an unlimited plan by design.
#
# Unset → the metered, usage, invoice and quota tests skip with a message.
# SCRIBE_SUBSCRIBED_SESSION_TOKEN=

# Alternative to the above: the customer alias of that workspace. The contract suite
# mints its own token from it using SCRIBE_SESSION_SECRET. Prefer the token if you
# have one.
# SCRIBE_SUBSCRIBED_CUSTOMER_ALIAS=

# Per-request timeout in ms for the contract suite. Meteroid-backed operations make
# one or two upstream calls. Default 20000.
# SCRIBE_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=20000

# How long the contract suite waits for Meteroid's eventually-consistent usage counters
# to catch up before asserting on them. Default 15000.
# SCRIBE_USAGE_SETTLE_MS=15000

# The SPA under test, for `tests/e2e` only. Vite's dev-server default.
# FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:5173

# Plan the e2e checkout spec starts a hosted checkout for. Must be a paid plan.
# SCRIBE_E2E_CHECKOUT_PLAN=pro
